    "result_text": "Only draft claims can be edited now, and a voided claim keeps its number. All 4 tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**Server** (`claim/ClaimService.java`)\n- Editing a claim that isn't a draft is refused with 409 before anything is written, so the store and audit file don't change. This covers settled and void claims too, since the request says only a draft can be edited.\n- Voiding no longer clears the claim number. Because the number stays on the claim, `CLAIM_VOIDED` ends with `number=N` when it was open. A draft that is voided still has no number, so its line has no number field, which is what the spec expects.\n- I updated the doc comments in `Claim.java` and `ClaimController.java` to match.\n\n**Front end** (`features/people/person-claims.ts`)\n- Pressing Edit on a claim that isn't a draft shows `claim-error` on that claim (\"A OPEN claim cannot be edited; void it and add a new one.\") and doesn't open the form.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **Server refusal still shows on the form:** if the screen is out of date (say another user opened the claim) and the edit reaches the server, the refusal appears as the form error, as before, not on the claim. I tried showing it on the claim, but a closed-period refusal also returns 409. With the current error responses the screen can't tell the two apart, so it could have shown the wrong message.\n- **Old voided claims stay unnumbered:** claims voided before this change already had their number cleared, and it can't be recovered, so they'll keep showing no number. No schema change was needed. The existing unique index on sheet and number still holds, because numbers are never reused.",
